{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by grant heslov, ben jaffee, and calvin joh. the film is based on the true story of the founding of facebook and the subsequent lawsuit between mark zuckerberg and the winklevoss twins.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a brilliant and reclusive college student who creates thefacebook, a social networking site that quickly gains popularity. the supporting cast includes andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, thefacebook's first president of finance; jacob batalon as divya Narendra, a friend who helps zuckerberg with the site; and armie hammer as cambridge university student androwdy riley, who claims to have come up with the idea for thefacebook.\n\none of the standout features of the film is its portrayal of the complex and often dark world of the tech industry. the film shows how zuckerberg's obsession with thefacebook drives him to make difficult decisions and sacrifice his relationships and his own well-being. at the same time, the film also highlights the genius and innovation that makes zuckerberg a success.\n\njessie eisenberg's performance as mark zuckerberg is particularly noteworthy. he brings a sense of vulnerability and intensity to the role, and his chemistry with the rest of the cast is excellent. andrew garfield is also excellent as eduardo saverin, bringing a sense of charm and charisma to the role that makes him both likable and relatable.\n\nthe film also features a number of memorable supporting performances, including armie hammer as cambridge university student. hammer brings a sense of arrogance and entitlement to the role, which makes him a compelling and believable character.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the film is its cinematography. david fincher's direction is known for its dark and moody aesthetic, 